    "final_message": "I built the Everbloom app with Exact, but it can't be built or run for iOS on this machine. Current Exact needs Xcode 27 (the iOS 27 SDK), and only Xcode 26.6 is installed here; with 26.6, compiling Exact's own iOS code fails. So none of the iOS items in the Done list are met yet: the device and simulator builds, the simulator walkthrough, the `SHOPBENCH home_ready` log line, and the accessibility IDs as seen by XCUITest.\n\nI tried to install Xcode 27.2 from the App Store, but that asks for an admin password, which I don't have. I also didn't want to replace the system Xcode, because the other framework projects on this machine use it. I had briefly made a copy of Xcode 26.6 as a fallback; I deleted it when the install failed, so the system is as it was.\n\n**What I could check:**\n- **Web:** I ran the same app code in Exact's web host (headless Chromium, phone-sized window, live mock.shop data). Every flow in the Done list worked, including Retry after the endpoint comes back. Checkout wasn't tried there, and neither was the alert for cart errors.\n- **Android:** the release APK builds, launches on the emulator, and Home loads.\n- **Tests:** the 3 tests in `everbloom/app.test.contract` pass on the web host. They cover the loading skeleton, tabs, empty search, empty cart and the error state.\n\n**What's untested:**\n- The iOS build scripts follow Exact's documented commands but haven't run end to end.\n- For `DEVELOPMENT_TEAM` signing, Exact builds the app without an Xcode project. The script therefore first builds a dummy Xcode project with the same bundle ID, so Xcode creates the team's provisioning profile, then has Exact sign with it. That dummy project builds with signing off; the signed path has never run.\n\n**Next step:** install Xcode 27 with admin rights (in parallel with Xcode 26.6 if the other projects need it), set `DEVELOPER_DIR` to point at it, and run `scripts/build-ios-simulator.sh`. After that I can do the simulator walkthrough and check the log line. iOS layout specifics such as safe areas haven't been seen on a real iOS build yet, so I'd expect a round of fixes there.\n\n**How it's laid out:**\n- **App folder:** the app is in `everbloom/`, not the project root. Exact names its build pieces after the folder, and `exact` clashes with Exact's own names, so it refused to create the app at the root.\n- **Exact and Ibex:** both are cloned into `vendor/` at pinned commits by `scripts/install.sh`, and Exact isn't modified. Ibex is Expo's Hermes repo; Android needs a Hermes build that no Ibex release ships yet, and the build takes about 10 minutes, once.\n- **Endpoint:** on iOS, `SHOP_API_URL` is read at launch by a small Swift module, which also writes the `os_log` line. On Android it's read at build time.\n- **Plain HTTP on Android:** it needed no setting. Exact makes requests over its own sockets, which Android's cleartext policy doesn't govern.\n- **Dependency:** the only one added is Exact's own `@exact/in-app-browser`, used for Check out.\n- **Extra machine setup:** to build, I installed Playwright's Chromium, Bun 1.4.2, the Android SDK platform 37.2, and the Rust iOS targets.\n\nAll my decisions where the spec is silent, plus the Xcode blocker, are in `NOTES.md`.\n\nFiles are in `/Users/itome/shopbench-runs/exact`:\n- `BUILD.md`\n- `NOTES.md`\n- `build/android/Everbloom-release.apk`"
